  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nForecast confidence is increasing for advisory level winds\ndeveloping later this week across eastern and southern Arizona.\nGuidance continues to show a fast moving dry shortwave trough\ndiving southwestward into the western part of the large scale\ntrough lingering across the Southwestern U.S. Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. This disturbance is then expected to form a cut- \noff low just to our southwest at the same time strong ridging \nedges into California and the Great Basin. \n\nThursday looks to be a transition day with a continuation of\nenhanced northerly winds down the Lower CO River Valley while\ntemperatures warm closer to normals as northerly downslope \nwarming spreads through much of Arizona. These northerly winds\nshould quickly shift out of the east late Thursday night/Friday \nmorning as the cut-off low forms and drifts southwestward off the \ncoast of Baja. A strong pressure gradient will then develop across\nArizona in response to strong surface high pressure building over\nthe southern Rockies and the Plains to the east and low pressure \nto our west. There may even be a distinguishable backdoor cold \nfront boundary that will move through Friday morning. Guidance \nshows 30-45 kt H8 winds setting up across southern Arizona Friday \nmorning which should translate to widespread 30-40 mph easterly gusts\nat times to as high as 45 mph across portions of Pinal and \nsouthern Maricopa Counties. A Wind Advisory is expected to be \nneeded for these areas on Friday and possibly into Saturday \nmorning before the gradient begins to relax.\n\nThe strong easterly winds on Friday will also usher in some cooler\nair from New Mexico, keeping daytime highs in the low to mid 80s\nacross the Phoenix area. The western lower deserts will be less\naffected by this back-door front with highs closer to 90 degrees.\n\nModel uncertainty increases for the weekend as there is some \nquestion of how long the cut-off low will sit just to our south \nbefore ejecting back to the east and whether or not the remnants \nof TC Simon will play any role in our weather. As of right now, \nguidance shows the cut-off weakening and shifting east northeast \nacross northern Mexico later Saturday into Sunday while the \nremnants of Simon mostly likely bypasses our region to the \nsoutheast. Before this occurs, the positioning of the cut-off low\nand the increased easterly flow is expected to bring good mid and\nupper level moisture advection into Arizona and southern \nCalifornia on Friday, but likely little if any low level moisture.\nThe most likely scenario calls for increased higher level clouds \non Saturday and a 10-20% chance of showers focused more toward \nsoutheast Arizona. However, there are some ensemble members (maybe\n20%) that try to pull better low level moisture (even potentially\nfrom Simon's remnants) into at least southeast Arizona Saturday \ninto early Sunday. If this were to occur, it would bring much \nbetter rain chances as far northwest as the Phoenix area. \nAlthough still unlikely, it will be something to watch over the \nnext few days. By early next week, we should have another ridge \nwith dry air advection moving in from the west leading to quieter \nweather conditions.",
